  // Section A — Social influence (24)
  Q(1, 1, 'A', 'A', MCQ('Which one of the following is most likely to lead to internalisation?', ['Wanting to be accepted by a group of strangers', 'Being unsure of the correct answer in an ambiguous situation', 'Being asked to conform by a person in uniform', 'Fearing ridicule if you give a different answer'], 'B'),
    AOs('AO1 = 1') + 'Answer: B', { mcq: true, ctx: [p('Only one answer is allowed.')] });
  Q(2, 3, 'A', 'A', 'Explain how task difficulty affected conformity in Asch’s research.',
    AOs('AO1 = 3') + '1 mark for identifying the effect, plus further marks for explanation.\n• When the task was made more difficult (comparison lines more similar to the standard line), conformity increased (1)\n• because the situation became more ambiguous / participants were less sure of the right answer (1)\n• so they looked to others for guidance – informational social influence increased (1)\nCredit other relevant material.');
  Q(3, 4, 'A', 'A', 'Use your knowledge of situational explanations of obedience to explain why Ama obeyed the instructions in the hospital but not in the car park.',
    AOs('AO2 = 4') + 'Level 2 (3–4 marks): Application of situational explanations is clear and effective; knowledge is linked explicitly to Ama’s behaviour.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Application is limited or muddled; links to the stem are weak.\nPossible application:\n• legitimacy of authority – in the hospital the doctor is high in the social hierarchy/has legitimate power given by society, so Ama accepts the order\n• uniform – the white coat and ID badge are visible symbols of authority; in the car park the man has no uniform\n• location – the hospital is a prestigious, official setting (like Yale in Milgram’s study); the car park lacks institutional authority\n• agentic state – in the hospital Ama sees herself as acting for the doctor, so does not feel responsible; in the car park she stays autonomous.\nCredit other relevant application.',
    { lv: true, ctx: [p('Ama is a hospital volunteer. A doctor in a white coat with an ID badge tells her to move a patient’s belongings to another ward, and she does so immediately. Later the same day, a man in jeans in the hospital car park tells her to move her car to a different space. Ama ignores him.')] });
  Q(4, 16, 'A', 'A', 'Discuss research into minority influence.',
    AOs('AO1 = 6, AO3 = 10') + 'Level 4 (13–16): Knowledge of research into minority influence is accurate and generally well detailed. Discussion is thorough and effective. The answer is clear, coherent and focused. Specialist terminology is used effectively. Minor detail and/or expansion of argument is sometimes lacking.\nLevel 3 (9–12): Knowledge is evident. Discussion is apparent and mostly effective. The answer is mostly clear and organised. Specialist terminology is mostly used effectively. Lacks focus in places.\nLevel 2 (5–8): Limited knowledge is present. Focus is mainly on description. Any discussion is of limited effectiveness. The answer lacks clarity, accuracy and organisation in places. Specialist terminology is used inappropriately on occasions.\nLevel 1 (1–4): Knowledge is very limited. Discussion is limited, poorly focused or absent. The answer lacks clarity, accuracy and organisation. Specialist terminology is either absent or inappropriately used.\n0: No relevant content.\nPossible content:\n• Moscovici et al. (1969): groups of six, two confederates; 36 blue slides; consistent minority said “green” – 8.42% agreement vs 1.25% inconsistent\n• consistency – synchronic and diachronic\n• commitment – augmentation principle\n• flexibility – Nemeth (1986): rigid minorities seen as dogmatic\n• process of conversion and snowball effect; internalisation.\nPossible discussion:\n• supporting research – Wood et al. (1994) meta-analysis of nearly 100 studies; Martin et al. (2003) – minority change more resistant to counter-persuasion\n• artificial tasks (slide colours) lack mundane realism – real minorities face bigger stakes\n• differences in power and status between minorities and majorities are rarely as large in lab groups\n• ethical issues (deception) and use of female students only\n• practical applications to understanding real social movements.\nCredit other relevant material.',
    { lv: true });

  // Section B — Memory (24)
  Q(5, 2, 'B', 'B', 'Distinguish between proactive interference and retroactive interference.',
    AOs('AO1 = 2') + '1 mark for each correct distinction made explicit:\n• proactive interference is when old/previously learned information disrupts the recall of new information\n• retroactive interference is when new information disrupts the recall of old information.\nAnswers must make the direction of interference clear for each mark.');
  Q(6, 4, 'B', 'B', 'Use your knowledge of explanations for forgetting to explain Toby’s experiences.',
    AOs('AO2 = 4') + 'Level 2 (3–4): Clear and effective application of explanations to both experiences.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Limited or muddled application; may only address one experience.\nPossible application:\n• Spanish then French: retroactive interference – the new French vocabulary disrupts recall of the earlier Spanish vocabulary because the material is similar\n• exam hall: retrieval failure / context-dependent forgetting – the cues present when Toby revised (his bedroom, music) are absent in the hall\n• encoding specificity – recall is best when the context at retrieval matches that at encoding\n• state-dependent – he revised calm but is anxious in the exam (internal cues differ).\nCredit other relevant application.',
    { lv: true, ctx: [p('Toby revised Spanish vocabulary on Monday and French vocabulary on Tuesday. In a Spanish test on Wednesday, he kept writing French words instead. He always revises in his bedroom with music playing, but in his exams in the school hall he finds it hard to remember things he knew well at home.')] });
  Q(7, 2, 'B', 'B', 'Explain why the researcher asked participants to count backwards in threes before recalling the letters.',
    AOs('AO2 = 2') + '• to prevent participants from rehearsing the letters (1)\n• so that the researcher measured the duration of STM without rehearsal / information was not kept in STM or transferred to LTM by rehearsal (1).',
    { sub: 1, ctx: [p('A researcher investigated the duration of short-term memory. Participants were shown three consonants (eg TXB), then counted backwards in threes from a random number for either 3, 9 or 18 seconds before recalling the letters. Each participant did all three conditions.'), p('Table 1 Mean percentage of letters correctly recalled after each interval'), grid(['Interval', '3 seconds', '9 seconds', '18 seconds'], [['Mean % correct', '86', '38', '9']])] });
  Q(7, 2, 'B', 'B', 'What do the results in Table 1 suggest about the duration of short-term memory? Use data in your answer.',
    AOs('AO2 = 2') + '• STM has a very short duration / information is quickly lost without rehearsal (1)\n• use of data, eg recall fell from 86% after 3 seconds to 9% after 18 seconds (1).', { sub: 2 });
  Q(7, 2, 'B', 'B', 'Identify one extraneous variable in this study and explain how it could have been controlled.',
    AOs('AO3 = 2') + '1 mark for an appropriate extraneous variable; 1 mark for an appropriate control.\n• order effects (practice/fatigue) – counterbalance the order of the conditions\n• different letter sets may be easier – use letter sets matched for difficulty / randomise\n• noise/distraction – conduct in a quiet, standardised room.\nCredit other relevant answers.', { sub: 3 });
  Q(8, 4, 'B', 'B', 'Outline the role of the central executive and the episodic buffer in the working memory model.',
    AOs('AO1 = 4') + '2 marks for each component: 1 for a basic point, 2 for a detailed/elaborated outline.\nCentral executive: supervisory/directs attention; allocates tasks to slave systems; very limited capacity; no storage; modality-free.\nEpisodic buffer: temporary store added in 2000; integrates information from the CE, PL, VSS and LTM; maintains time sequencing; limited capacity (about four chunks).');
  Q(9, 8, 'B', 'B', 'Evaluate the multi-store model of memory.',
    AOs('AO3 = 8') + 'Level 4 (7–8): Evaluation is thorough and effective; clear, coherent; specialist terminology used effectively.\nLevel 3 (5–6): Evaluation is mostly effective; mostly clear; terminology mostly used.\nLevel 2 (3–4): Evaluation is of limited effectiveness; lacks clarity in places.\nLevel 1 (1–2): Evaluation is very limited, poorly focused.\n0: No relevant content.\nNote: description of the MSM is not credited unless used as part of evaluation.\nPossible discussion:\n• research support for separate STM and LTM – Baddeley (1966) coding; HM; serial position effect\n• STM is not a unitary store – KF (Shallice & Warrington, 1970)\n• LTM is not unitary – different types (eg Clive Wearing) – note this is evaluation only\n• rehearsal is not always needed for transfer – elaborative processing (Craik & Watkins, 1973)\n• research uses artificial materials (consonant syllables) – lacks mundane realism\n• influential model, led to the WMM.\nCredit other relevant evaluation.', { lv: true });

  // Section C — Attachment (24)
  Q(10, 1, 'C', 'C', MCQ('Which one of the following is a feature of insecure-avoidant attachment?', ['High stranger anxiety', 'Resisting comfort on reunion', 'Little distress when the caregiver leaves', 'Using the caregiver as a secure base'], 'C'),
    AOs('AO1 = 1') + 'Answer: C', { mcq: true });
  Q(11, 2, 'C', 'C', 'Outline what Lorenz’s research showed about imprinting.',
    AOs('AO1 = 2') + '• goslings imprinted on (followed) the first moving object they saw after hatching – Lorenz (1)\n• this only occurred within a critical period of a few hours / imprinting influenced later mate preferences (sexual imprinting) (1).');
  Q(12, 3, 'C', 'C', 'Explain how Harlow’s research challenges learning theory as an explanation of attachment.',
    AOs('AO3 = 3') + '• learning theory claims infants attach to whoever feeds them (cupboard love) (1)\n• Harlow found monkeys preferred/clung to the cloth mother rather than the wire mother that provided milk, especially when frightened (1)\n• this suggests contact comfort, not food, is the basis of attachment, so learning theory is challenged (1).');
  Q(13, 2, 'C', 'C', 'Identify two behaviours observed in Ainsworth’s Strange Situation.',
    AOs('AO1 = 2') + '1 mark for each: proximity seeking; exploration/secure-base behaviour; stranger anxiety; separation anxiety; reunion behaviour.');
  Q(14, 16, 'C', 'C', 'Discuss Bowlby’s monotropic theory of attachment. Refer to Leon and Mia in your answer.',
    AOs('AO1 = 6, AO2 = 4, AO3 = 6') + 'Level 4 (13–16): Knowledge of Bowlby’s theory is accurate and generally well detailed. Application is effective. Discussion is thorough and effective. Minor detail and/or expansion of argument is sometimes lacking. The answer is clear, coherent and focused. Specialist terminology is used effectively.\nLevel 3 (9–12): Knowledge is evident. Application/discussion is apparent and mostly effective. The answer is mostly clear and organised. Specialist terminology mostly used effectively.\nLevel 2 (5–8): Limited knowledge is present. Focus is mainly on description. Any application/discussion is of limited effectiveness. The answer lacks clarity, accuracy and organisation in places.\nLevel 1 (1–4): Knowledge is very limited. Application/discussion is limited, poorly focused or absent. Specialist terminology absent or inappropriately used.\nPossible content:\n• attachment is innate and adaptive – promotes survival\n• monotropy – one special primary attachment; law of continuity; law of accumulated separation\n• social releasers (eg smiling, crying) trigger caregiving\n• critical period around 2½ years\n• internal working model – template for later relationships; continuity hypothesis.\nPossible application:\n• Leon’s smiling and babbling are social releasers that trigger caregiving from his father\n• Leon shows a monotropic attachment to his father – the person who responds most sensitively, not the person who feeds him most\n• Mia’s caring relationships with her own friends may reflect her secure IWM; her worry about the nursery relates to the law of accumulated separation\n• Leon is 9 months – within the critical period.\nPossible discussion:\n• support for social releasers – Brazelton et al. (1975)\n• support for IWM – Bailey et al. (2007)\n• challenge to monotropy – Schaffer & Emerson (1964): multiple attachments, some to fathers first\n• socially sensitive – may blame mothers who work (Burman, 1994)\n• temperament hypothesis (Kagan) as an alternative\n• critical period now seen as a sensitive period (Rutter’s ERA).\nCredit other relevant material.',
    { lv: true, ctx: [p('Leon is 9 months old. He smiles and babbles whenever his father picks him up, and cries when his father leaves the room, even though his mother does most of the feeding. Leon’s mother, Mia, has close and trusting friendships. She is worried that sending Leon to nursery full-time will harm his development.')] });

  // Section D — Clinical Psychology and Mental Health (24)
  Q(15, 3, 'D', 'D', 'Identify one behavioural, one emotional and one cognitive characteristic of OCD shown by Ravi. Refer to the description of Ravi in your answer.',
    AOs('AO2 = 3') + '1 mark for each characteristic correctly identified and linked to the stem:\n• behavioural – compulsion: washes his hands up to 40 times a day / avoids door handles\n• emotional – anxiety/distress when he cannot wash / feeling disgusted by germs\n• cognitive – obsessive thoughts about contamination / knows the thoughts are excessive (insight).',
    { ctx: [p('Ravi washes his hands up to 40 times a day and avoids touching door handles. If he cannot wash, he feels extremely anxious and disgusted. He constantly thinks about germs, although he says he knows his worries are “over the top”.')] });
  Q(16, 3, 'D', 'D', 'Outline the statistical infrequency definition of abnormality.',
    AOs('AO1 = 3') + '• behaviour/characteristics that are statistically rare/uncommon in the population are abnormal (1)\n• for normally distributed characteristics, scores more than 2 standard deviations from the mean are considered abnormal (1)\n• example, eg IQ below 70 is one criterion for intellectual disability (1).');
  Q(17, 2, 'D', 'D', 'Explain one difference between flooding and systematic desensitisation.',
    AOs('AO1 = 2') + '1 mark for identifying a difference; 1 mark for explaining/elaborating it.\n• flooding involves immediate exposure to the most feared stimulus, whereas SD involves gradual exposure via an anxiety hierarchy\n• SD uses relaxation (reciprocal inhibition), whereas flooding relies on extinction / exhaustion of the fear response\n• flooding is typically one long session; SD takes several sessions.');
  Q(18, 16, 'D', 'D', 'Discuss the cognitive approach to explaining and treating depression.',
    AOs('AO1 = 6, AO3 = 10') + 'Level 4 (13–16): Knowledge of the cognitive approach to explaining and treating depression is accurate and generally well detailed. Discussion is thorough and effective. Clear, coherent, focused. Specialist terminology used effectively.\nLevel 3 (9–12): Knowledge evident; discussion apparent and mostly effective. Mostly clear and organised.\nLevel 2 (5–8): Limited knowledge; mainly description; limited discussion.\nLevel 1 (1–4): Very limited knowledge; discussion limited, poorly focused or absent.\nPossible content:\n• Beck’s negative triad – negative views of the self, world and future; faulty information processing; negative self-schemas\n• Ellis’s ABC model – activating event, irrational beliefs (musturbation, I-can’t-stand-it-itis), consequences\n• CBT – identifying and challenging irrational thoughts; reality testing; disputing (logical, empirical, pragmatic); homework; behavioural activation.\nPossible discussion:\n• supporting evidence – Cohen et al. (2019): cognitive vulnerability in 473 adolescents predicted later depression\n• the explanations do not explain all aspects (eg extreme anger, hallucinations, Cotard delusion)\n• Ellis explains reactive depression better than depression without an obvious trigger\n• CBT effectiveness – March et al. (2007): 81% improved with CBT, 81% drugs, 86% combined\n• CBT less suitable for severe depression / low motivation; may blame the client and minimise life circumstances\n• therapist–client relationship may be what matters (Rosenzweig – dodo bird verdict)\n• comparison with biological treatments (eg SSRIs).\nCredit other relevant material.',
    { lv: true });

